18
00:01:14,149 --> 00:01:18,070
and optimization when it comes to ML workloads.

19
00:01:18,070 --> 00:01:20,469
That's a great thing for you to be working on.

20
00:01:20,469 --> 00:01:27,030
because today we will be talking about low latency AI applications.

21
00:01:27,030 --> 00:01:31,429
Before we get into latency in AI in particular,

22
00:01:31,429 --> 00:01:36,710
let's talk about generally in applications when we're talking about latency, what are we talking about?

23
00:01:36,710 --> 00:01:38,789
So when we talk about latency.

24
00:01:38,789 --> 00:01:43,350
What we really care about is what the user experiences the moment they try to do something.

25
00:01:43,350 --> 00:01:45,670
To the moment they actually experience the response.

26
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:51,030
So it's entirely from the side of the person interacting with the system.

27
00:01:51,030 --> 00:01:54,630
Not so much from the side of like the service.

28
00:01:54,630 --> 00:01:57,270
or the background or how much we're able to process all at once.

29
00:01:57,280 --> 00:02:05,030
You could imagine a system in which a another computer program is interacting with the system.

30
00:02:05,030 --> 00:02:08,389
Does your definition rule out calling it latency in those cases?

31
00:02:08,399 --> 00:02:10,309
No.

32
00:02:10,309 --> 00:02:12,229
You can think about latency kind of in all sorts of cases.

33
00:02:12,229 --> 00:02:17,910
where all you're measuring is the time from something to start to the time that something finishes.

34
00:02:17,910 --> 00:02:20,470
And so it's basically just elapsed time of some kind of operation.

35
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:24,790
And a lot of times I just tend to think about it from what the user experiences.

36
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:31,750
Cuz that is the type of latency that a lot of software applications care to optimize.

37
00:02:31,750 --> 00:02:36,630
Maybe one example that I think about is people joke about the AI pause nowadays.

38
00:02:36,630 --> 00:02:38,869
When you're having a voice call with chat GBT voice.

39
00:02:38,869 --> 00:02:41,030
Where it pauses for three or 4 seconds.

40
00:02:41,030 --> 00:02:44,949
That time span is what I think of as latency in a lot of user interactive applications.

41
00:02:44,959 --> 00:02:49,750
We'll definitely talk about that in a bit.

42
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:53,030
I want to do some more general purpose talk concepts about latency.

43
00:02:53,030 --> 00:02:59,430
We did a podcast on this some years ago that was talking about consumer applications.

44
00:02:59,430 --> 00:03:04,790
And the guest presented some data about human behavior in response to latency.

45
00:03:04,790 --> 00:03:10,710
Showing that people are highly sensitive to both average latency and tail latency.

46
00:03:10,710 --> 00:03:13,510
Even down into milliseconds. Now this was years ago.

47
00:03:13,510 --> 00:03:21,911
Do you have any updated research or your own ideas about what humans experience as a noticeable latency? Yeah.

48
00:03:21,911 --> 00:03:24,229
So, it depends a lot on the application.

49
00:03:24,229 --> 00:03:28,710
So, there are some really prominent ones like superhuman mail,

50
00:03:28,710 --> 00:03:33,110
which is an email client, where what they've optimized for is the latency of every interaction.

51
00:03:33,110 --> 00:03:37,350
And they found that when responding to keystrokes, for example.

52
00:03:37,350 --> 00:03:42,949
You click a shortcut in the application, how long until the mail app updates based on that.

53
00:03:42,949 --> 00:03:45,190
The target was under 100 milliseconds.

54
00:03:45,190 --> 00:03:47,190
Anywhere between 50 and 100 milliseconds would feel good.

55
00:03:47,190 --> 00:03:51,270
and anything above 100 started to feel bad very quickly.

56
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:54,470
It's a little bit different for kind of traditional software.

57
00:03:54,470 --> 00:03:58,630
and a lot more of the AI applications and what the specific interaction is.

58
00:03:58,630 --> 00:04:03,910
So like in voice cases it tends to be 500 to 600 milliseconds tends to be tolerable.

59
00:04:03,910 --> 00:04:08,390
In UIs, if you hit more than 30 to 40 milliseconds for response to like a user hitting.

60
00:04:08,390 --> 00:04:13,429
a button, then that starts to feel laggy very very fast.

61
00:04:13,429 --> 00:04:18,310
And people can perceive latency down to like 5 or 10 milliseconds.

62
00:04:18,310 --> 00:04:23,030
Like if my keystrokes when I input them into an app lag by 10 milliseconds,

63
00:04:23,030 --> 00:04:27,749
I tend to use those kinds of notepad editors way less than other ones.

64
00:04:27,749 --> 00:04:34,310
Do you have any thoughts on whether people are responding to average latency,

65
00:04:34,310 --> 00:04:37,270
longtail latency, worst case latency or some other aggregator?

66
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:45,830
I think people tend to measure latency based on the 95th to 99th percentile experience that they have.

67
00:04:45,830 --> 00:04:51,030
Like whenever at whisper people have mentioned that things feel slow.

68
00:04:51,030 --> 00:04:54,710
When we look at the distribution of times they get it's actually usually one.

69
00:04:54,710 --> 00:04:56,550
or two outlier cases where it feels slow.

70
00:04:56,550 --> 00:05:00,790
And so people tend to measure latency based on their worst perceived experience.

71
00:05:00,790 --> 00:05:02,950
Not based on their average perceived experience.

72
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:10,469
In trying to engineer the obvious cause of average latency is the system has to do some work.

73
00:05:10,469 --> 00:05:13,111
for you in order to give you a response. And that takes time.

74
00:05:13,111 --> 00:05:18,323
you can try to make it run faster, but longtail latency may have.

75
00:05:18,333 --> 00:05:20,469
[clears throat]

76
00:05:20,469 --> 00:05:26,070
different causes. And do you have any experience in tracking down where that originates?

77
00:05:26,070 --> 00:05:30,950
So that's where the hardest challenges in latency come from?

78
00:05:30,950 --> 00:05:35,909
Because when you think about P99 latency, it's like 100 different things that can cause the issue.

79
00:05:35,909 --> 00:05:40,070
And it's about figuring out how to find those and diagnose those.

80
00:05:40,070 --> 00:05:42,870
So for us, one of the ways.

81
00:05:42,870 --> 00:05:47,271
that we think about and analyze P99 latency is looking at the impact of the network on the. user.

82
00:05:47,271 --> 00:05:49,510
Networks are super stochastic and so every once in a while,

83
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:54,150
a request just won't make it where it should get to.

84
00:05:54,150 --> 00:06:00,150
And the way that we try to track these things down is by making sure.

85
00:06:00,150 --> 00:06:02,629
that we have as much observation as possible.

86
00:06:02,629 --> 00:06:04,629
Into the breakdown of the different parts of the latency.

87
00:06:04,629 --> 00:06:07,110
So if we measure the time for the work, right?

88
00:06:07,110 --> 00:06:10,870
As you said, that's the time that every request is going to have to take.

89
00:06:10,870 --> 00:06:13,749
And the time for every single sequence of steps.

90
00:06:13,759 --> 00:06:15,350
What we can do is we can figure out,

91
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:20,790
okay, this hop of network latency was the source of the issue.

92
00:06:20,790 --> 00:06:22,950
And then we can try and figure out how do we get.

93
00:06:22,950 --> 00:06:25,350
that 99th percentile latency to go down.

94
00:06:25,350 --> 00:06:30,390
Do you have any stories of when you looked at this telemetry.

95
00:06:30,390 --> 00:06:35,350
and you found something maybe you weren't expecting that was taking up an normal amount of time?

96
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:38,071
Absolutely. So this is really common.

97
00:06:38,071 --> 00:06:40,950
So we host models for voice dictation.

98
00:06:40,950 --> 00:06:43,749
So basically users will press a button on their computer, they're going to speak.

99
00:06:43,759 --> 00:06:48,629
We send it to a server for transcription where we host all of the models ourselves.

100
00:06:48,629 --> 00:06:51,749
And then we return a response to the user.

101
00:06:51,749 --> 00:06:54,950
And we run both a speech model and an LLM as part of that pipeline.

102
00:06:54,950 --> 00:06:57,029
That's to kind of give context on the application where we've seen it.

103
00:06:57,039 --> 00:07:02,550
The most unexpected one that has come up for us is we found.

104
00:07:02,550 --> 00:07:04,309
that things were making it to the server fast.

105
00:07:04,309 --> 00:07:08,070
Things were getting processed by the server fast,

106
00:07:08,070 --> 00:07:13,431
but one day all of a sudden all of our response times went up by about 300 to. 400 milliseconds.

107
00:07:13,431 --> 00:07:18,870
And what we found in that case was the time between the first model running.

108
00:07:18,870 --> 00:07:21,510
and the second model running suddenly had a spike.

109
00:07:21,510 --> 00:07:28,230
And the reason that we eventually uncovered is something in our deployment allowed one GPU to be in.

110
00:07:28,230 --> 00:07:30,230
one region and the other GPU to be in a different region.

111
00:07:30,230 --> 00:07:33,990
So we would go to California for processing our audio.

112
00:07:33,990 --> 00:07:38,710
Then we would somehow send it to the Midwest for processing the LLM and all the post-processing.

113
00:07:38,710 --> 00:07:42,550
And this was kind of opaque behindthe-scenes infrastructure things.

114
00:07:42,550 --> 00:07:46,950
where we thought everything was getting deployed to the same place.

115
00:07:46,950 --> 00:07:48,550
And then we looked under the hood.

116
00:07:48,550 --> 00:07:50,790
and we found that okay there's like network responses going on here.

117
00:07:50,790 --> 00:07:53,909
And then we were able to diagnose it and bring it all back into the same region.

118
00:07:53,919 --> 00:08:00,309
What's interesting about that story, one of the things you wanted to talk about was latency and scale.

119
00:08:00,309 --> 00:08:05,029
That story illustrates how as you have a larger system.

120
00:08:05,029 --> 00:08:10,710
and you either are scaling out for reasons of handling more traffic or other reasons, it also impacts your latency.

121
00:08:10,720 --> 00:08:13,510
Absolutely.

122
00:08:13,510 --> 00:08:17,670
Like every time you expand a system, the kinds of places where you can incur latency.

123
00:08:17,670 --> 00:08:19,830
Where you route traffic or you get it to the right place.

124
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:22,230
or your databases have higher load on it.

125
00:08:22,240 --> 00:08:25,670
Start to very quickly crop up.

126
00:08:25,670 --> 00:08:31,110
And often what happens is they kind of hit a tipping point at different parts of scale.

127
00:08:31,110 --> 00:08:32,949
So things will look fine for a really long time,

128
00:08:32,949 --> 00:08:35,909
then all of a sudden, bam, you'll expose a new source of latency.

129
00:08:35,909 --> 00:08:39,190
And you're going to have to spend a bunch of time figuring that one out, optimizing it.

130
00:08:39,190 --> 00:08:41,269
And building the system for that growing scale.

131
00:08:41,269 --> 00:08:47,110
Do you advise or at your company do you institute latency regression testing?

132
00:08:47,120 --> 00:08:57,269
The way that we have done it is primarily looking at analytics data from day-to-day.

133
00:08:57,269 --> 00:09:03,430
And so we'll have analytics on latency for every event that users experience with our application.

134
00:09:03,430 --> 00:09:06,630
And we will of course test things before we promote the deployments,

135
00:09:06,630 --> 00:09:11,350
but we'll basically do gradual rollouts of new infrastructure as we roll it out.

136
00:09:11,350 --> 00:09:14,070
And we will measure impact on latency there.

137
00:09:14,080 --> 00:09:16,710
And so we can see on any given day,

138
00:09:16,710 --> 00:09:20,949
on any hour of any day, did the latency suddenly spike for any reason?

139
00:09:20,949 --> 00:09:24,710
We can see when we do new rollouts and new deployments,

140
00:09:24,710 --> 00:09:27,350
did it bring down the latency as we would expect it to?

141
00:09:27,350 --> 00:09:31,269
But we haven't done any specific kind of regression testing there.

142
00:09:31,269 --> 00:09:37,190
Maybe these systems we build now are so complex and impossible to really simulate in staging.

143
00:09:37,200 --> 00:09:41,269
That you can't exactly test them before you roll them out.

144
00:09:41,269 --> 00:09:45,990
But you can detect when you have a problem quickly and resolve it.

145
00:09:45,990 --> 00:09:48,550
That's very much the approach that we take.

146
00:09:48,550 --> 00:09:53,750
and that I think a lot of fastmoving companies in the AI space take right now.

147
00:09:53,750 --> 00:09:55,829
Because so many of these workloads are really stochastic.

148
00:09:55,839 --> 00:09:58,230
They're super random.

149
00:09:58,230 --> 00:10:02,070
different things happen on GPUs that are extremely non-predictable.

150
00:10:02,070 --> 00:10:09,591
and non-deterministic and so making sure that you can immediately respond to any spike that's the most important. thing for us.

151
00:10:09,591 --> 00:10:16,310
So hedge, we've been talking about latency for a bit, converging on the intersection of latency and AI.

152
00:10:16,310 --> 00:10:20,949
Let's talk about what AI is briefly.

153
00:10:20,949 --> 00:10:24,310
The way I observe this term, it's a bit of a moving target.

154
00:10:24,310 --> 00:10:30,150
where it tends to overindex on what happened in the last 5 minutes.

155
00:10:30,150 --> 00:10:32,870
But we've had AI for decades now.

156
00:10:32,870 --> 00:10:40,790
If we're going to be talking about AI and latency, what domains of AIs are we talking about?

157
00:10:40,790 --> 00:10:44,870
I mean latency becomes important in any of these kinds of applications.

158
00:10:44,870 --> 00:10:51,590
So whether it's more classical techniques that are classifiers that are trained on certain kinds of data.

159
00:10:51,590 --> 00:10:54,389
Which are often used in recommendation systems and ads models.

160
00:10:54,389 --> 00:11:01,750
Or whether it's larger and larger ML workloads like large speech models or large language models, right?

161
00:11:01,750 --> 00:11:04,389
which are models that have billions of parameters.

162
00:11:04,399 --> 00:11:11,910
And in those kinds of cases, because they're running so much computation for any given request.

163
00:11:11,910 --> 00:11:17,190
That's the reason why latency has become such a hot topic in this kind of a community.

164
00:11:17,190 --> 00:11:22,470
Where before maybe we're running 20,000 floatingoint operations for getting one result back.

165
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:29,430
Now we might be running 10 billion or 100 billion floatingoint operations to get one result back.

166
00:11:29,430 --> 00:11:34,069
And as soon as you start to have to do that much computation,

167
00:11:34,069 --> 00:11:38,870
that's when optimizing these systems for a different type of latency constraint starts to become relevant.

168
00:11:38,880 --> 00:11:46,630
Could you give an example of something using AI and what the latency expectations would be?

169
00:11:46,630 --> 00:11:49,590
Something that's not a large language model.

170
00:11:49,590 --> 00:11:58,470
So for not a large language model tools using AI include basically all the recommendation systems.

171
00:11:58,470 --> 00:12:00,710
that people use for the Twitter algorithm, the Facebook algorithm.

172
00:12:00,710 --> 00:12:03,030
For showing you different things.

173
00:12:03,030 --> 00:12:09,990
And in those kinds of cases, there's actually usually a cascade of different models that are running.

174
00:12:09,990 --> 00:12:14,949
Like first you'll use a model to retrieve a bunch of candidates of things.

175
00:12:14,949 --> 00:12:17,750
that you might want to show the user.

176
00:12:17,750 --> 00:12:21,430
Then you actually rank those things.

177
00:12:21,430 --> 00:12:25,110
And so each stage of each model has a different latency requirement to like retrieve them.

178
00:12:25,110 --> 00:12:27,350
You might be operating on saying, hey,

179
00:12:27,350 --> 00:12:31,750
which of these like 10,000 posts are even potentially relevant to the user.

180
00:12:31,750 --> 00:12:36,471
And so that model has to be super super fast to be able to run on all those. things.

181
00:12:36,471 --> 00:12:39,030
Then the next model might say, hey,

182
00:12:39,030 --> 00:12:43,509
of these 10 things, what's the thing that I show in the top place to the user?

183
00:12:43,509 --> 00:12:45,269
And with a lot of these systems,

184
00:12:45,269 --> 00:12:51,030
the way people get creative is you can show people the first page of results really fast.

185
00:12:51,030 --> 00:12:55,590
And then you can have more time before the user scrolls to compute things.

186
00:12:55,590 --> 00:12:57,350
that are going to show up on the second page.

187
00:12:57,350 --> 00:13:05,110
And so that's where like people really optimize for like first page load latency versus what you see.

188
00:13:05,110 --> 00:13:09,670
after and can buy more time for running certain computations more efficiently.

189
00:13:09,670 --> 00:13:14,310
This reminds me of another question I had about latency.

190
00:13:14,310 --> 00:13:19,590
where you can define it as the time from when you request until you start getting results.

191
00:13:19,590 --> 00:13:22,470
Is the thinking behind this last point.

192
00:13:22,470 --> 00:13:28,069
if you can start showing the reader some results whether it's LLM chat.

193
00:13:28,069 --> 00:13:30,150
or recommendations and we read pretty slow.

194
00:13:30,150 --> 00:13:32,870
So the reader's going to start reading.

195
00:13:32,870 --> 00:13:34,870
And you have time now where you're ahead of the reader.

196
00:13:34,870 --> 00:13:37,110
And you can fetch more results.

197
00:13:37,110 --> 00:13:40,470
And the reader doesn't perceive it as lag. Is it something like that?

198
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:42,871
That's exactly right. Yeah.

199
00:13:42,871 --> 00:13:47,590
And it's taking advantage of the fact that we're engaging with the content.

200
00:13:47,590 --> 00:13:51,269
And so we have plenty more time to run computation in the background.

201
00:13:51,269 --> 00:13:52,790
While the user is actively engaged.

202
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:56,389
We discussed these findings.

203
00:13:56,389 --> 00:14:01,350
I recall a study that was covered in the news about Google.

204
00:14:01,350 --> 00:14:03,750
If they introduced a 100 milliseconds delay in search.

205
00:14:03,750 --> 00:14:08,550
that people almost stopped using Google for 3 months or something.

206
00:14:08,550 --> 00:14:10,870
And it wasn't even a perceptible amount.

207
00:14:10,870 --> 00:14:15,350
It was something subconscious where the person feels like I'm having a bad experience.

208
00:14:15,350 --> 00:14:21,350
Yet with these LLMs, people will sit there for seconds waiting for an answer.

209
00:14:21,350 --> 00:14:28,629
Why is this tolerated when people seem to have such a short attention span in other domains?

210
00:14:28,639 --> 00:14:30,710
It's a great question.

211
00:14:30,710 --> 00:14:33,829
I think it comes back to two different things.

212
00:14:33,829 --> 00:14:39,670
So one like the immediate value that I get out of a LLM query is.

213
00:14:39,670 --> 00:14:42,710
so high in so many cases.

214
00:14:42,710 --> 00:14:44,550
Like I'm immediately getting the final answer that I need.

215
00:14:44,550 --> 00:14:50,550
If you contrast it to Google, I've asked a question, I got 100 milliseconds. Now I have a bunch of search results.

216
00:14:50,550 --> 00:14:52,389
that I now have to go click through to even see.

217
00:14:52,399 --> 00:14:54,629
if I find the thing that I want to.

218
00:14:54,629 --> 00:15:01,189
And so there's so many steps involved there to get to the thing that I actually want to.

219
00:15:01,189 --> 00:15:05,830
Whereas with chat GPT like if it's going to take 2 seconds for me to get a magical. answer.

220
00:15:05,830 --> 00:15:07,430
That answers the world's most complex question.

221
00:15:07,430 --> 00:15:10,790
That I previously didn't have a way to get an answer to, I'll wait.

222
00:15:10,790 --> 00:15:14,069
So that's like one part of it.

223
00:15:14,069 --> 00:15:16,710
And the other part is I think the user expectation that has been set.

224
00:15:16,710 --> 00:15:18,870
So once people get used to a certain thing,

225
00:15:18,880 --> 00:15:25,189
it becomes very easy for them to continue working with that kind of model.

226
00:15:25,189 --> 00:15:29,829
But like I mentioned this earlier with the AI pause on voice mode, for example, on chat GPT.

