676
00:45:47,109 --> 00:45:51,671
Because I can't send every single one of those requests to a server. So that's one example.

677
00:45:51,671 --> 00:45:53,910
A lot of vision models, this is really important.

678
00:45:53,920 --> 00:45:59,829
So vision models will often want to run on my phone or on my device.

679
00:45:59,829 --> 00:46:02,230
If for example I'm looking through a camera app.

680
00:46:02,240 --> 00:46:06,711
and I want to tag objects that I'm looking at. Or if Tesla is driving a car,

681
00:46:06,720 --> 00:46:10,230
the amount of compute they can actually run on the device in the car.

682
00:46:10,230 --> 00:46:15,990
Is going to be lower than what they can train when they're training their models with their servers.

683
00:46:15,990 --> 00:46:18,390
And so, you know, any of these kinds of things.

684
00:46:18,390 --> 00:46:23,829
where you don't want to be reliant on a network for being able to make a request.

685
00:46:23,829 --> 00:46:29,349
And then you don't have to run servers as a developer yourself.

686
00:46:29,349 --> 00:46:31,910
You can have everything run on the user's device.

687
00:46:31,910 --> 00:46:35,430
And then for privacy sensitive applications, it's kind of the final core one where it's hey,

688
00:46:35,430 --> 00:46:37,030
no data will leave the device.

689
00:46:37,030 --> 00:46:40,631
It runs right there. And that's super useful.

690
00:46:40,631 --> 00:46:47,430
AI are LLMs in particular are different in the amount of randomness they introduce into the response.

691
00:46:47,430 --> 00:46:49,990
What are some techniques for managing that?

692
00:46:49,990 --> 00:46:55,270
This is one of the hardest parts about serving a lot of these applications.

693
00:46:55,270 --> 00:46:57,990
And one of the ways in which.

694
00:46:57,990 --> 00:47:00,069
that occurs is the kind of classic problem of hallucinations in AI.

695
00:47:00,079 --> 00:47:03,190
So maybe you ask a question and.

696
00:47:03,190 --> 00:47:07,910
then all of a sudden the model goes off in a crazy loop.

697
00:47:07,910 --> 00:47:11,270
And then starts to even do bad things like repeat the same sentence over.

698
00:47:11,270 --> 00:47:13,109
and over and over again in an answer.

699
00:47:13,109 --> 00:47:16,150
And then the request just never stops, never terminates.

700
00:47:16,150 --> 00:47:19,990
And keeps consuming resources, never giving the user what they want.

701
00:47:19,990 --> 00:47:25,109
There's a bunch of different pathologies like this that can show up when serving AI applications.

702
00:47:25,119 --> 00:47:30,390
And there's two or three important things to do to handle this.

703
00:47:30,390 --> 00:47:32,950
So the first is like safeguards.

704
00:47:32,950 --> 00:47:34,790
So if you know how long an answer should be,

705
00:47:34,790 --> 00:47:38,710
that at least lets you cut off how bad of an output you can generate.

706
00:47:38,710 --> 00:47:40,790
So that's one thing that's really helpful.

707
00:47:40,790 --> 00:47:47,510
The second thing is catching it as the model is producing it.

708
00:47:47,510 --> 00:47:51,349
So one thing that can be done is like if the model starts hallucinating going off the rails.

709
00:47:51,349 --> 00:47:55,750
Start answering a different question from what it was asked.

710
00:47:55,750 --> 00:47:59,910
That can be caught part way because the output's being partially produced.

711
00:47:59,910 --> 00:48:01,829
And heristic algorithms can be used to catch that.

712
00:48:01,829 --> 00:48:03,510
Other LLM could be used to catch that.

713
00:48:03,510 --> 00:48:08,150
All of these kinds of things allow for handling those kinds of problems.

714
00:48:08,150 --> 00:48:10,230
And so you know this becomes really important.

715
00:48:10,230 --> 00:48:12,630
Because it's like even with normal questions.

716
00:48:12,630 --> 00:48:15,589
that a user might ask sometimes these systems just go off the rails.

717
00:48:15,599 --> 00:48:20,790
And as a developer, it's important that you're the one responsible for making.

718
00:48:20,800 --> 00:48:22,790
sure the system doesn't go off the rails.

719
00:48:22,800 --> 00:48:24,710
When a user is asking a normal question,

720
00:48:24,720 --> 00:48:28,470
let alone for the kind of handful of users who will try to break your system.

721
00:48:28,480 --> 00:48:32,630
And give it hard things that will cause those kinds of problems to occur.

722
00:48:32,640 --> 00:48:38,390
Now, in the last few minutes, I want to talk about the issues of scale.

723
00:48:38,390 --> 00:48:44,230
How does latency degrade or become increasingly challenging at scale?

724
00:48:44,230 --> 00:48:46,549
So scale has a tremendous amount of impact on latency.

725
00:48:46,559 --> 00:48:53,510
And I think a lot of it has to do with how GPU requests tend to.

726
00:48:53,510 --> 00:48:56,069
actually get processed.

727
00:48:56,069 --> 00:48:59,270
The more and more a load is on the GPU.

728
00:48:59,270 --> 00:49:01,030
So GPUs work by parallelizing lots of operations.

729
00:49:01,030 --> 00:49:03,430
They run lots of computations in parallel.

730
00:49:03,430 --> 00:49:08,790
But if two people, for example, submit a request at the same time.

731
00:49:08,790 --> 00:49:11,030
And mine has a, you know, 200word question with a 10-word answer.

732
00:49:11,040 --> 00:49:16,230
And yours has a 10-word question with a 200word answer.

733
00:49:16,230 --> 00:49:19,829
And the GPU tries to process them simultaneously.

734
00:49:19,829 --> 00:49:23,750
It now all of a sudden has to do two slightly different things for each of us.

735
00:49:23,750 --> 00:49:28,630
And so the way in which it can optimize the parallel computation changes, right?

736
00:49:28,630 --> 00:49:31,990
And so there's a lot of things.

737
00:49:31,990 --> 00:49:34,069
that occur as systems and requests start to scale.

738
00:49:34,079 --> 00:49:38,549
where more and more different types of requests get batched.

739
00:49:38,549 --> 00:49:43,349
And on the flip side, you have more opportunity to optimize it, right?

740
00:49:43,349 --> 00:49:46,390
So I can try as somebody working on infrastructure to optimize it.

741
00:49:46,390 --> 00:49:48,870
So similar kinds of requests go to the same server, right?

742
00:49:48,880 --> 00:49:53,349
And so that gives me opportunities for optimizing latency in a way.

743
00:49:53,349 --> 00:49:56,230
that wasn't possible when there wasn't scale.

744
00:49:56,230 --> 00:50:00,309
And so scale both helps and hurts when it comes to latency.

745
00:50:00,309 --> 00:50:04,470
The place where it'll probably hurt the most is if you ever run out of resources.

746
00:50:04,470 --> 00:50:08,150
And then now you're dealing with higher and higher load on the same set of machines.

747
00:50:08,150 --> 00:50:10,710
And doing your best to make sure that you can actually service everybody.

748
00:50:10,720 --> 00:50:20,070
And how much of this behaviors that are more um unique to GPUs do you know going into. it?

749
00:50:20,070 --> 00:50:22,549
And how much have you discovered with observability.

750
00:50:22,549 --> 00:50:27,030
and digging into things that were not behaving as you expected?

751
00:50:27,040 --> 00:50:29,829
I'd probably say 50/50.

752
00:50:29,829 --> 00:50:31,510
Like there's a bunch of these kinds of problems.

753
00:50:31,510 --> 00:50:36,710
that we know going in where we haven't solved them yet.

754
00:50:36,710 --> 00:50:40,150
And we know that at the next batch of scale it's going to be important to solve them.

755
00:50:40,150 --> 00:50:44,230
And there's a bunch of things that come up that are extremely hard to predict.

756
00:50:44,230 --> 00:50:50,390
Because sometimes one flag that you set for an NVIDIA CUDA kernel is the kind of thing.

757
00:50:50,400 --> 00:50:58,470
That changes the performance of a system by 25% under a specific load pattern that we're experiencing.

758
00:50:58,470 --> 00:50:59,990
Like we literally had that happen at one point.

759
00:51:00,000 --> 00:51:06,790
where one CUDA kernel flag improved performance by 25% due to a specific load pattern.

760
00:51:06,790 --> 00:51:11,829
And that's the kind of thing where there's no way we could have ever guessed.

761
00:51:11,829 --> 00:51:14,950
that going into building the system.

762
00:51:14,950 --> 00:51:16,870
But by observing it, by seeing when it spiked,

763
00:51:16,870 --> 00:51:22,870
and then by looking at profiling of, hey, these operations were taking this amount of time.

764
00:51:22,870 --> 00:51:26,630
We're able to figure out that that's the thing that we needed to optimize in that situation.

765
00:51:26,640 --> 00:51:33,670
Are there any other lessons learned or best practices around managing latency that you'd like to convey?

766
00:51:33,680 --> 00:51:38,950
I think the most important one is that for anything latency sensitive,

767
00:51:38,960 --> 00:51:46,069
everything that's not critical should get removed from the critical path. And you know, when I phrase it that way,

768
00:51:46,069 --> 00:51:50,150
it sounds obvious cuz uh it's called the critical path.

769
00:51:50,150 --> 00:51:55,829
But it's really easy to have a database query here or a blocking operation there.

770
00:51:55,829 --> 00:52:00,710
That is occurring in the primary path for a latency sensitive application.

771
00:52:00,710 --> 00:52:02,470
But 3 milliseconds here, 5 milliseconds there,

772
00:52:02,470 --> 00:52:07,030
10 milliseconds another place and that's under good conditions,

773
00:52:07,030 --> 00:52:09,589
you know, could become 100 milliseconds here or there really fast.

774
00:52:09,599 --> 00:52:16,710
And it's actually often the innocuous pieces of code.

775
00:52:16,710 --> 00:52:22,550
that people write that can very easily just cause overhead that doesn't need to be there. Right?

776
00:52:22,550 --> 00:52:28,630
That's the easy kind of overhead to avoid when being disciplined about programming.

777
00:52:28,630 --> 00:52:31,750
A lot of the other stuff is like you're gonna have to whack away at it.

778
00:52:31,750 --> 00:52:33,430
It's going to be a hard problem.

779
00:52:33,430 --> 00:52:38,549
You're going to have to dive into logs, observe things,

780
00:52:38,549 --> 00:52:40,710
and understand how like kernels work and things of that sort.

781
00:52:40,710 --> 00:52:44,950
And that's the hard work that always needs to happen.

782
00:52:44,950 --> 00:52:48,390
But the best practice of like make.

783
00:52:48,390 --> 00:52:52,549
sure only the absolute bare minimum needs to happen in any blocking operation on the critical path.

784
00:52:52,549 --> 00:52:55,109
Like that's the kind of golden rule for me.

785
00:52:55,119 --> 00:52:59,190
It sounds very much like an idea.

786
00:52:59,190 --> 00:53:03,990
that I ran across some years ago under the name of reactive applications.

787
00:53:03,990 --> 00:53:10,069
Where the idea was anything that you don't need the response in line put it in an async.

788
00:53:10,069 --> 00:53:13,510
And just let it happen on its own.

789
00:53:13,510 --> 00:53:15,829
And if you need to know when it finished use an event.

790
00:53:15,839 --> 00:53:19,990
Exactly. Okay.

791
00:53:19,990 --> 00:53:21,750
Well, Sahed thank you so much for this conversation.

792
00:53:21,750 --> 00:53:25,910
Before we wrap up, would you like to direct listeners to anywhere on the internet.

793
00:53:25,910 --> 00:53:28,470
where they can find either you or your company?

794
00:53:28,480 --> 00:53:32,710
Yeah, so I would just recommend going to the Whisper Flow website.

795
00:53:32,710 --> 00:53:34,470
So that's where you can see some of our research.

796
00:53:34,480 --> 00:53:41,829
and our blogs about how we think about optimizing and building voice AI models for improving things.

797
00:53:41,829 --> 00:53:46,470
And you can also download and play around with the application.

798
00:53:46,470 --> 00:53:48,069
which lets you speak instead of type anywhere on your computer.

799
00:53:48,069 --> 00:53:51,750
So that's, you know, the best set of resources.

800
00:53:51,750 --> 00:53:54,549
And then I have a small lightweight personal blog that you can find online as well.

801
